So.  I've been playing NH for about seven years now, on and off; but
I've tended towards some of the more delicate classes, and had been
generally playing it more to have fun than focusing on ascension.
(Also, I usually just play under whatever my username is, but I
decided I wasn't feeling valkyrieish enough that way, so changed it.)
But, eventually, I figured I'd try something a little different.  This
is the end result of that (with a few YASDs along the way, of course).

This game was played kind of intermittently over most of a month, and
I didn't think to take notes, so I don't have a nice little narrative,
but I'll try to hit the more "interesting" parts; it was fairly
ordinary in many respects, all told.  (I also sort of dragged my feet
on writing and posting this for, oh, two weeks now; but there I have
an excuse, as I was playing more NH instead.)

I think the closest thing to early-game "excitement" was a polymorph
trap, but an elf mummy is almost the same size as a dwarf, so no armor
was lost.  It did, however, lose me the no-polyself conduct; aside
from that, no-polypile and no-artifact-wish are the only ones I kept
that the game cares about.  I also refrained from tactical use of the
E-word, causing conflict, or using a rubber chicken; and I didn't
reverse-genocide or farm.

Forward genocides were L (on general principle), both sizes of mind
flayer as they turned up, and R when something in Orcus-town read a
?oCM and got a disenchanter that I didn't feel like being subtle with.
I spared ; as I never had any problem with them; I'd just hit them a
few times and they'd die and that would be that.  Likewise, I didn't
get much trouble from the Wizard -- sure, he showed up 11 times, but
was fairly easily handled in melee.

I delayed doing Sokoban (which I normally just skip) until I'd gotten
to the Castle with neither reflection nor a bag, and decided that
either one would be worth it (see my earlier post on that); and I
didn't do Ludios until after a few levels of Gehennom, as I'd been
*quite* convinced of the value of clairvoyance at that point.  (Also,
I'd sort of half-forgotten Ludios was there.)

Wishes, all from the Castle wand: scrolls of charging, a GDSM, a
[oRefl, an "oLS, a magic marker (mostly for ?oEA), a =oFA (to replace
the one that got blown up by trying to throw Mjollnir while blinded by
a soldier with a wand of lightning in the side of the Castle), and a
t-shirt.

"Fun" moment #1: the bottom level of the quest had the stairs
separated from everything else by lava, which I had no way to freeze.
But I did have a couple !oLev, so I floated over and went to deal with
Lord Surtur.  On the way back, predictably, a fire trap took out the
other !oLev, because I hadn't yet found any bags.  Oops.  I was a bit
distraught -- which was actually good, because I didn't notice when
another fire trap blew up my !oConf, so that when I read a ?oTele out
of semi-desperation, it became level teleport, which is what I needed.
I'd forgotten about that feature, you see.

"Fun" moment #2: stumbling into one of the Wizard's moats, more than
once, because I'd gone to zap a wand and not noticed the "Nothing
happens."  before keying the direction.  While there were also a few
typos-into-water earlier, this was especially irritating in that it
blanked the b?oRC I'd gone to the trouble of writing.  (But I still
had many !oHW, and I would up not getting much in the way of curses.
The blessed genocide of L may have helped there.)

"Fun" moment #3: killing the Wizard over part of his moat. With,
again, nothing to freeze the water to extract the Book (actually, in
hindsight I think I could have wrested a charge).  And no ?oEarth,
either; I think I'd used my last to get in.  But I *did* have some
blank scrolls and six whole marker charges, which is apparently enough
for a 75% chance at writing a ?oEarth.  That worked, and boulders
rained down, and... failed to fill the square with the Book.  So then
I got to do a little diagonal watery mini-Sokoban to maneuver one of
the free boulders into that spot.

"Fun" moment #4: I left the Invocation Artifacts down in the Sanctum,
as I had no further use for them, and they have weight.  Or at least
that's what I *thought* I'd done; but, when I finally got back to the
Castle, I noticed I still held the Bell, and the Orb of Fate was
nowhere to be found.  Oops.  I was about to despair, but then realized
I could just set the Amulet down and levelport to get the Orb.

"Fun" moment #5: towards the end of the ascent through the main
dungeon, going to altarize a few items of unknown beatitude --
including some potions.  While still levitating from Medusa or so.
TDTTOE, including what happens when you drop fragile objects from up
in midair onto a hard surface.

"Fun" moment #6: on the Astral plane (where I couldn't be bothered
with /oTele or other subtlety), I spent a while being protected from
Pestilence by the intercession of a bee, which stood between us.  

Anyway.  Away we go:


